{"name":"Blonde Wool","hex":"#e5d0b1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blonde","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of blond (“person with fair hair”).","Alternative form of blond (“color”)"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative form of blond.","Stupid, ignorant, naive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blonde"},{"term":"wool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hair of the sheep, llama and some other ruminants.","A cloth or yarn made from such hair."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wool"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blond","blocks":[{"text":"Blond or blonde, also referred to as fair hair, is a human hair color characterized by low levels of eumelanin, the dark pigment. The resultant visible hue depends on various factors, but always has some yellowish color. The color can be from the very pale blond to reddish \"strawberry\" blond or golden-brownish (\"sandy\") blond colors. Occasionally, the state of being blond, and specifically the occurrence of blond traits in a predominantly dark or colored population are referred to as blondism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Lars-Unnerstall2.jpg/330px-Lars-Unnerstall2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blond"},{"title":"Wool","blocks":[{"text":"Wool is the textile fiber obtained from sheep and other mammals, especially goats, rabbits, and camelids. The term may also refer to inorganic materials, such as mineral wool and glass wool, that have some properties similar to animal wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/330px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wool"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Anonymous_F